                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "PeterYinGit",
                        "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "PeterYinGit"
                    },
                    "message": "please refer to here: https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/84735"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "PeterYinGit",
                        "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "PeterYinGit"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Battery voltage isn't in PSUSensor, that would be in ADCSensor, so I'm a little confused why this use case matters?  Is this just a problem of us putting a device in the wrong sensor and would be solved by moving the device to the correct daemon?\n\nWhy do they \"require\" different monitoring frequencies?  This sensor daemon is for PMBus based sensors, which are cached in the kernel, so it's not clear why this changes abn individual polling rate would change the actual processing to the device."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Can you specify which device you're intending to change here?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> Battery voltage isn't in PSUSensor, that would be in ADCSensor, so I'm a little confused why this use case matters?\n\nI don't think that is 100% true.  One implementation _is_ to use the ADC, but another is to use a remote sensor device that is covered by PSUSensor.  For example an INA233, which is a current monitor, can be coupled with a known resistor size to determine a voltage.\n\nFrom the commit message, it does appear as those we ended up with some multi-channel ADC devices (attached via i2c) being surfaced by the PSUSensor daemon.  Maybe the originator can document the specific chips they're attempting to use this feature on.\n\n> Why do they \"require\" different monitoring frequencies? \n\nA battery you want to read at a far slower frequency than most sensors.  In order to read a battery, most implementation require some \"jumper\" enabled via a GPIO that hooks the battery up to a drain.  If you leave that set all the time, or sense too often, you end up draining the battery.  I think typically we only read battery sensors approximately once per day.\n\n> This sensor daemon is for PMBus based sensors, which are cached in the kernel, so it's not clear why this changes abn individual polling rate would change the actual processing to the device.\n\nThe kernel driver for a device _may_ cache a reading, but the kernel never reads _on its own_.  It is always driven by an access to the pmbus sysfs entries.  If a sensor daemon is the only thing reading a sensor, and the sensor daemon only reads once a day, the kernel will only read the device once a day."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "PeterYinGit",
                        "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "PeterYinGit"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, we use the ADC128D818 to monitor the battery voltage sense, so we hope the PSU sensor can support per-channel polling rate settings."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I believe this is resolved.  @ed@tanous.net, if you want some specific changes to the commit message, please let the author know."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "ACK, yes, the above explanation is reasonable."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Actually, read through again, the question about PSUSensor vs HWMonTempSensor doesn't seem to be answered.  ADC128D818 looks like this device was added to the PSUSensor backend (application meant for PMBus) instead of being added to HWmonTemp sensor.  PMBus devices are expected to have one poll rate due to being paged, that's why only one is allowed.  Hwmon devices are intended to be polled independently, which is why it includes multiple poll rates.\n\nThose two applications are used interchangeably because the kernel abstracts all the readings away from userspace, but I suspect that's the root of the issue here."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Is your suggestion that we move ADC128D818 to ... HWMonTemp instead?\n\n```\n$ rg ADC128            \nsrc/psu/PSUSensorMain.cpp\n67:    {\"ADC128D818\", I2CDeviceType{\"adc128d818\", true}},\n```\n\n\nThis would require changes back in entity-manager to migrate that chip specifically from PSUSensor to hwmon."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 16,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, I understand this path would require two patchsets not one.  I just want to understand if the thought of PSUSensor = pmbus, hwmon = hwmon was considered when this change was made, and if that weighs into adding features that only need to exist because that categorization wasn't groked here.\n\nWhen I review devices, I have to mostly assume folks added them to the right application because I don't have time to look up every datasheet.  I did in this case and this doesn't look like a pmbus device.\n\nYes, the compatibility of configs is a concern, but in theory removing the type from psusensor and adding to hwmon should be accomplishable.  Whether it's worth it idk, I just want to understand the thought process;  We should probably get the distinction documented somewhere so that we don't end up in this situation again."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Same as above, this is per-device, not global"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "@peter.yin@quantatw.com - It looks like Ed is asking for a minor change to the commit message here.  Whenever you've said \"global\", the code seems to actually support a PollRate property from the EM config to support per-device intervals already.  So it isn't a global rate."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "PeterYinGit",
                        "email": "peter.yin@quantatw.com",
                        "username": "PeterYinGit"
                    },
                    "message": "change to default PollRate."
                }
            ],
